@@ category: Sliding Window

@@@ problem 209 minimum-size-subarray-sum
title: Minimum Size Subarray Sum
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Binary Search, Sliding Window, Prefix Sum
params: target, nums
time: O(n)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1

=== description
Given an array of **positive** integers `nums` and a positive integer `target`, return the minimal length of a contiguous subarray whose sum is **greater than or equal to** `target`. If there is no such subarray, return `0`.

=== constraints
- `1 <= target <= 10^9`
- `1 <= nums.length <= 10^5`
- `1 <= nums[i] <= 10^4`

=== hints
- All numbers are positive, so extending a window increases its sum and shrinking decreases it.
- Grow the window on the right; while the sum is big enough, record the length and shrink from the left.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int minSubArrayLen(int target, vector<int>& nums) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::minSubArrayLen);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        auto v = g.ints(n, 1, 10000);
        long long total = 0;
        for (int x : v) total += x;
        long long t = g.coin(0.1) ? total + g.irange(1, 100) : g.range(1, std::max(1LL, total / std::max(1, g.irange(1, 40))));
        return lc::lines((int)std::min<long long>(t, 1000000000), v);
    });
}

=== approach
**Variable-size sliding window.** Extend the right end one element at a time, adding it to `sum`. As long as `sum >= target`, the current window is a candidate: record its length, then remove the leftmost element to see if a shorter window still works. Each element enters and leaves the window at most once, so the scan is O(n).

This works only because all numbers are positive (the sum is monotone in the window size).

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int minSubArrayLen(int target, vector<int>& nums) {
        int best = INT_MAX;
        long long sum = 0;
        int left = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < (int)nums.size(); ++right) {
            sum += nums[right];                 // grow the window
            while (sum >= target) {             // window is valid: try to shrink it
                best = min(best, right - left + 1);
                sum -= nums[left++];
            }
        }
        return best == INT_MAX ? 0 : best;
    }
};

=== examples
7
[2,3,1,2,4,3]
> 2
: The subarray [4,3] has the minimal length.

4
[1,4,4]
> 1

11
[1,1,1,1,1,1,1,1]
> 0
: Even the whole array sums to only 8.

=== tests
1
[1]
> ?

5
[1]
> ?

15
[1,2,3,4,5]
> ?

11
[1,2,3,4,5]
> ?

@@@ problem 3 longest-substring-without-repeating-characters
title: Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters
difficulty: Medium
topics: Hash Table, String, Sliding Window
params: s
time: O(n)
space: O(min(n, alphabet))
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=s.length

=== description
Given a string `s`, return the length of the longest **substring** (contiguous) that contains no repeated characters.

=== constraints
- `0 <= s.length <= 5 * 10^4`
- `s` consists of English letters, digits, symbols and spaces

=== hints
- Keep a window with no repeats. When the next character is already inside the window, where must the window's left edge jump to?
- Remember the last index at which each character was seen.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int lengthOfLongestSubstring(string s) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::lengthOfLongestSubstring);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        const std::string pools[] = {"ab", "abcde", "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789 !?"};
        return lc::lines(g.str(n, pools[g.irange(0, 2)]));
    });
}

=== approach
Slide a window `[left, i]` that never contains a repeated character. Store `last[c]`, the most recent index of each character. When we read `s[i]` and its last occurrence is inside the window (`last[c] >= left`), move `left` just past it. The window is then repeat-free again, and its length `i - left + 1` is a candidate.

With a fixed-size table for the 128 ASCII characters, the extra space is constant.

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    int lengthOfLongestSubstring(string s) {
        vector<int> last(128, -1);   // last index where each ASCII char was seen
        int best = 0, left = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < (int)s.size(); ++i) {
            unsigned char c = s[i];
            if (last[c] >= left) left = last[c] + 1;   // repeat inside window: jump past it
            last[c] = i;
            best = max(best, i - left + 1);
        }
        return best;
    }
};

=== examples
"abcabcbb"
> 3
: "abc" is the longest substring without repeats.

"bbbbb"
> 1

"pwwkew"
> 3
: "wke". Note "pwke" is a subsequence, not a substring.

=== tests
""
> ?

" "
> ?

"au"
> ?

"dvdf"
> ?

"abba"
> ?

"tmmzuxt"
> ?

@@@ problem 30 substring-with-concatenation-of-all-words
title: Substring with Concatenation of All Words
difficulty: Hard
topics: Hash Table, String, Sliding Window
params: s, words
time: O(n · L)
space: O(k)
bench: n=64..262144 time=n space=1 unit=s.length_(L_=_3,_k_=_4)

=== description
You are given a string `s` and an array `words` of strings that all have the **same length** `L`.

A **concatenated string** is any string formed by concatenating all of `words` in some order (each word used exactly as many times as it appears). Return the starting indices of all substrings of `s` that are concatenated strings. The order does not matter (the judge sorts them).

=== constraints
- `1 <= s.length <= 10^4`
- `1 <= words.length <= 5000`
- `1 <= words[i].length <= 30`
- `s` and `words[i]` consist of lowercase English letters

=== hints
- Every match starts at an offset `r` in `0..L-1` and then proceeds in steps of `L`.
- For each offset, slide a window over whole words, keeping counts of the words inside the window.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> findSubstring(string s, vector<string>& words) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::findSubstring).sort_output();
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        int L = n <= 40 ? g.irange(1, 3) : 3, k = n <= 40 ? g.irange(1, 4) : 4;
        std::vector<std::string> words;
        for (int i = 0; i < k; ++i) words.push_back(g.lower(L, 2));
        std::string s;
        while ((int)s.size() < n) {
            if (g.coin(0.3)) { auto w = words; g.shuffle(w); for (auto &x : w) s += x; }
            else s += g.lower(g.irange(1, 3), 2);
        }
        s.resize(std::max(n, 1));
        return lc::lines(s, words);
    });
}

=== approach
Let `L` be the word length and `k` the number of words; a match has length `L·k`.

Any match starts at some offset `r` in `0..L-1` and consists of consecutive `L`-sized chunks. For each offset, **slide a window over chunks**:

- read the next chunk; if it's not a word, clear the window and restart after it;
- otherwise add it to `seen`; while that word now appears more often than in `words`, drop chunks from the left;
- when the window holds exactly `k` chunks, record its start.

Each chunk is added and removed at most once per offset, giving O(n · L) time (hashing a chunk costs O(L)).

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> findSubstring(string s, vector<string>& words) {
        vector<int> result;
        int L = words[0].size(), k = words.size(), n = s.size();
        unordered_map<string, int> need;             // word -> required count
        for (auto &w : words) need[w]++;

        for (int r = 0; r < L; ++r) {                // each alignment separately
            unordered_map<string, int> seen;         // counts inside the window
            int left = r, count = 0;                 // count = #words in window
            for (int j = r; j + L <= n; j += L) {
                string w = s.substr(j, L);
                auto it = need.find(w);
                if (it == need.end()) {              // not a word: restart after it
                    seen.clear();
                    count = 0;
                    left = j + L;
                    continue;
                }
                seen[w]++;
                count++;
                while (seen[w] > it->second) {       // too many copies of w: shrink
                    seen[s.substr(left, L)]--;
                    left += L;
                    count--;
                }
                if (count == k) {                    // window is a permutation
                    result.push_back(left);
                    seen[s.substr(left, L)]--;       // slide by one word
                    left += L;
                    count--;
                }
            }
        }
        return result;
    }
};

=== examples
"barfoothefoobarman"
["foo","bar"]
> [0,9]
: "barfoo" starts at 0 and "foobar" starts at 9.

"wordgoodgoodgoodbestword"
["word","good","best","word"]
> []

"barfoofoobarthefoobarman"
["bar","foo","the"]
> [6,9,12]

=== tests
"a"
["a"]
> ?

"aaaaaa"
["aa","aa"]
> ?

"wordgoodgoodgoodbestword"
["word","good","best","good"]
> ?

"lingmindraboofooowingdingbarrwingmonkeypoundcake"
["fooo","barr","wing","ding","wing"]
> ?

"ababab"
["ab","ba"]
> ?

@@@ problem 76 minimum-window-substring
title: Minimum Window Substring
difficulty: Hard
topics: Hash Table, String, Sliding Window
params: s, t
time: O(n + m)
space: O(1)
bench: n=64..1048576 time=n space=1 unit=s.length

=== description
Given strings `s` (length `n`) and `t` (length `m`), return the **shortest substring** of `s` that contains every character of `t`, **including duplicates**. If there is no such substring, return `""`.

If several windows have the minimal length, any of them is accepted (the judge reports the leftmost one).

Follow-up: can you do it in O(n + m) time?

=== constraints
- `1 <= n, m <= 10^5`
- `s` and `t` consist of English letters

=== hints
- Count how many of each character you still need, plus how many characters are still missing in total.
- Expand the right edge until nothing is missing, then shrink from the left while the window stays valid.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    string minWindow(string s, string t) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.custom([](lc::Case &c) {
        std::string s = c.arg<std::string>(), t = c.arg<std::string>();
        const std::string S = s, T = t;  // copies for validation (made before timing)
        std::string r;
        Solution sol;
        c.time([&] { r = sol.minWindow(std::move(s), std::move(t)); });
        // Any minimal window is accepted, so print a canonical one: the leftmost
        // valid window with the returned length. Invalid answers print as-is.
        int needT[128] = {0};
        for (char ch : T) needT[(unsigned char)ch]++;
        auto covers = [&](const std::string &w) {
            int cnt[128] = {0};
            for (char ch : w) cnt[(unsigned char)ch & 127]++;
            for (int k = 0; k < 128; ++k) if (cnt[k] < needT[k]) return false;
            return true;
        };
        if (r.empty() || S.find(r) == std::string::npos || !covers(r)) { c.out(r); return; }
        size_t L = r.size();
        int cnt[128] = {0};
        long missing = (long)T.size();
        for (size_t i = 0; i < S.size(); ++i) {
            unsigned char in = S[i];
            if (cnt[in] < needT[in]) --missing;
            ++cnt[in];
            if (i >= L) {
                unsigned char outc = S[i - L];
                --cnt[outc];
                if (cnt[outc] < needT[outc]) ++missing;
            }
            if (i + 1 >= L && missing == 0) { c.out(S.substr(i + 1 - L, L)); return; }
        }
        c.out(r);
    });
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        std::string alpha = g.coin() ? "ABCabc" : "ABCDEFGHab";
        std::string s = g.str(n, alpha);
        std::string t = g.str(g.irange(1, std::min(6, std::max(1, n))), alpha.substr(0, 4));
        return lc::lines(s, t);
    });
}

=== approach
Keep `need[c]`: how many more copies of `c` the window still needs (negative = surplus), and `missing`: the total number of characters of `t` not yet covered.

1. Move `right` forward. If `need[s[right]] > 0` this character was useful, so `missing--`. Then `need[s[right]]--`.
2. When `missing == 0` the window is valid. Shrink it from the left: while the leftmost character is surplus (`need[s[left]] < 0`), give it back and advance `left`. Now the window is minimal for this `right`; record it.
3. To keep searching, drop one needed character from the left (`missing++`) and continue.

Both pointers only move forward, so the total time is O(n + m).

=== solution
class Solution {
public:
    string minWindow(string s, string t) {
        int need[128] = {0};
        for (char c : t) need[(unsigned char)c]++;
        int missing = t.size();               // characters of t not yet in the window
        int bestStart = 0, bestLen = INT_MAX;
        int left = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < (int)s.size(); ++right) {
            unsigned char c = s[right];
            if (need[c] > 0) missing--;       // this char was still required
            need[c]--;
            if (missing == 0) {
                // Drop surplus characters from the left.
                while (need[(unsigned char)s[left]] < 0) need[(unsigned char)s[left++]]++;
                if (right - left + 1 < bestLen) {
                    bestLen = right - left + 1;
                    bestStart = left;
                }
                // Give up the leftmost needed char and look for the next window.
                need[(unsigned char)s[left++]]++;
                missing++;
            }
        }
        return bestLen == INT_MAX ? "" : s.substr(bestStart, bestLen);
    }
};

=== examples
"ADOBECODEBANC"
"ABC"
> "BANC"
: "BANC" contains 'A', 'B' and 'C'.

"a"
"a"
> "a"

"a"
"aa"
> ""
: t needs two 'a's but s has only one.

=== tests
"ab"
"b"
> ?

"abc"
"cba"
> ?

"aa"
"aa"
> ?

"bba"
"ab"
> ?

"cabwefgewcwaefgcf"
"cae"
> ?
